# The room to shift supply between parts without moving any composed figure is what the layer graph
# says it is. One row.
#
# The identity
#   A part belongs to one fusion. Each fusion's room is one fewer than its parts, so the room across
#   the corpus is the number of parts less the number of fusions. The layer graph counts the same
#   parts as edges, so the two counts are read from each end, and neither side is free to move
#   alone.
#
# Unconditional
#   Where the layer graph has no loops, the identity can be read off the layers at the bottom and
#   the separate pieces of the graph. That form is not the one asserted here: making this law
#   depend on the graph having no loops would make it abstain exactly when the graph got
#   interesting, and `algebra/cycle_space.sqlc` already holds the loops at zero.
#
# What it can catch, which is not the arithmetic
#   One fewer per fusion, added up, is the parts less the fusions for any grouping, so what this law
#   checks is the crossing: that the grouping's count of parts is the graph's count of edges.
#   `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` joins a layer's `(filing, layer)` into one string on a `/`, and
#   `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` counts the edges `DISTINCT (graph, filing, from_node, to_node)`
#   afterwards. Joining into one string keeps layers apart on this corpus by luck rather than by
#   design, and it is the one place that has to be true. Two layers colliding under it, or one
#   fusion naming one layer twice, drops the edge count below the part count, and this law is what
#   notices.
#
# Its three companions
#   This one is the room on the part side. `algebra/composition_row_space.sqlc` is the one way each
#   fusion's figure moves, beside it; `algebra/composition_image.sqlc` is what the parts can reach
#   on the fusion side; and `algebra/composition_closure.sqlc` is what becomes of this count once
#   compositions nest. Four laws over one composition map.
#
# Not passing on nothing
#   A corpus with no fusions satisfies the identity as nought equals nought. The floor requires
#   parts, fusions and edges before the comparison counts, so an empty corpus reports that it
#   examined nothing rather than that the model holds.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `composition_kernel`, by collapsing every part layer of one filing
#   onto the filing's own name in the composed SQL: the edge count falls and the part count does
#   not. Dropping the `DISTINCT` in `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` moves nothing instead, because the
#   corpus files no edge twice, so it is not a probe of anything.
-- rank/composition_kernel.sqlc gathered, against rank/graph_measures.sqlc's layer row.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/composition_kernel' AS subject,
           x.parts > 0 AND x.fusions > 0 AND x.edges > 0
           AND x.parts = x.edges
           AND x.kernel = x.edges - x.fusions                                        AS holds,
           format('room for %s move(s) over %s part(s) in %s fusion(s); the layer graph has '
                  '%s edge(s), %s node(s), %s component(s) and %s independent loop(s)',
                  x.kernel, x.parts, x.fusions, x.edges,
                  x.nodes, x.components, x.dim)                                      AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT k.parts, k.fusions, k.kernel,
               m.m_edges AS edges, m.n_nodes AS nodes,
               m.c_components AS components, m.cycle_space_dim AS dim
        FROM      (
            SELECT coalesce(sum(c.parts), 0)      AS parts,
                   count(*)                       AS fusions,
                   coalesce(sum(c.kernel_dim), 0) AS kernel
            FROM ( :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc) ) c
        ) k
        CROSS JOIN (
            :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
        ) m
        WHERE m.graph = 'layers' AND m.filing IS NULL
    ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'composition_kernel'
